            <ArticleTitle>[Treatment of coronary pseudoaneurysm by stent-graft implantation].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>418-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY AND CLINICAL FINDINGS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 54-year-old man was urgently admitted because of sudden onset of progressively worsening angina pectoris, his first attack. Physical examination was unremarkable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Electrocardiography and laboratory tests excluded acute myocardial infarction. With the exception of hypercholesterolemia (total cholesterol 247 mg/dl) laboratory tests were normal. Coronary angiography revealed a 60% eccentric narrowing in the proximal part of the interventricular branch with adjacent aneurysmatic dilatation. Intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) showed a coronary pseudoaneurysm, its cavity communicating with the empty atheroma hole of an adjacent ruptured coronary plaque.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT AND COURSE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 19 mm stent graft was implanted, via a percutaneously inserted balloon-catheter system, in the region of the stenosis and the pseudoaneurysm. Subsequent angiography demonstrated a smooth nonstenotic lumen. The membrane of the graft (made of polytetrafluoroethylene [PTFE]), fixed between two thin metal stents, had occluded the pseudoaneurysm. Occlusion of an immediately distal septal branch briefly produced an asymptomatic rise of creatine kinase to maximally 173 U/l. Oral medication included ticlopidine hydrochloride (2 x 250 mg daily for 4 weeks). The patient was symptom-free after the procedure and was discharged 5 days later.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Implantation of a new type of stent-graft provides quick and uncomplicated treatment of a coronary aneurysm. The membrane fixed between two stents prevents wash out of any thrombi. The method may also be applicable to other potentially thrombus-containing lesions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
